SM(237,{"n":"Genesis 13.14-14.16","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Genesis 13.14-14.16.mp3","d":2778.6,"s":[[0.24,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in Biblical Studies, and is an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[22.48,"Verse 14. Then God said to Abram after Lot separated from him, Lift your eyes now and look from the place where you are, northward, southward, eastward, and westward."],[35.04,"For all the land you see, I give to you and your seed forever."],[39.68,"Then I will make your seed as the dust of the earth, that if a man could number the dust of the earth, then your seed also could be numbered."],[49.2,"Arise, walk in the land through its length and its breadth, for I will give it to you."],[53.68,"Then Abram moved his tent and went and dwelt by the Oak of Mamre in Hebron and built an altar there to the Lord."],[59.52,"There's altar number three"],[65.32,"Near Hebron, which is going to be in Judah's territory."],[71.32,"Later. And he goes to stay by the Oak of Mamre."],[77.48,"We'll come back to the Oak of Mamre."],[78.92,"Mamry's a person."],[81.72,"This is an already impressive oak at this point that is on his an already ancient oak tree that is on his property that serves as a landmark."],[93.05,"And by the fact that it's included here in Genesis, remember, we've been seeing these names that date to later periods showing up here in Genesis."],[102.81,"The fact that it's mentioned, this oak means this oak is still there later on when the Israelites get there."],[111.91,"Post-Joshua. So when Caleb and Subcame to Hebron, this oak is there."],[117.04,"This is being pointed out because you can still go there and see that oak."],[120.8,"This is where Abraham stayed."],[123.52,"This is that altar that Abram built."],[127.09,"This is this is that spot."],[130.29,"And the identification of these spots, we mentioned a little bit the issue of a single sanctuary, multiple sanctuaries last time in terms of the altars."],[138.69,"But another important thing is just these geographical locales, and their importance."],[145.43,"And we're going to keep seeing this going forward."],[149.76,"This shows something about religion as it's presented in the Torah."],[158.18,"This shows that it has a reference to specific places."],[164.02,"The particular places in 3D space that you can actually go, are relevant, religiously."],[172.69,"Meaning you're gonna have like pilgrimage sites."],[177.49,"People would go to see this oak."],[179.81,"They would go to see this oak, and part of that would be recounting what had happened there."],[184.37,"More is gonna happen there later."],[185.89,"But what had happened there in Abram's life?"],[189.01,"That was was part part. of their faith, that was part of their heritage, that of their inheritance"],[195.85,"So there's an idea of sacred space already here in Genesis."],[201.61,"Because God is the God of what?"],[204.97,"As we talked about last time, he's the God of Abram."],[210.17,"So that makes these important places in the life of Abram."],[216.15,"Places that are holy or sacred to the God of Abram."],[221.03,"That Isaac and Jacob later on."],[224.9,"So God comes to be associated with these places, and these places become identified as sacred because he has identified himself with Abram, and these places become important in Abram's life."],[237.55,"With God. So some of our friends from other Christian traditions don't like the idea of sacred space like this, and of pilgrimage."],[249.15,"Some of them will decry it and then go to the Holy Land anyway."],[254.19,"Um I've told my parents about the church of the Holy Sepulchre before."],[263.63,"And uh they didn't believe it was real."],[266.65,"Yeah. They're I mean, Southern Baptist sound as well."],[271.01,"Yeah. They didn't believe it was it's like, oh, we don't actually know what the June is."],[276.41,"Like really. So this is this is a built-in thing to ancient Israelite religion to to Judaism to Christianity."],[288.66,"That this is this idea of particular places where particular things have being significant."],[298.9,"But you don't realize it."],[300.18,"Like if anytime you go into like any kind of uh like a historic landmark or a battle view, yeah on a tour, the tour is telling you a story of the place."],[310.64,"Yeah. What happened, particularly in the early 19th century, is that the formerly religious sensibility became attached to political realities."],[328.62,"So we even have ritual."],[331.02,"Yeah. Yeah. Yeah."],[334.94,"So you get the Lincoln Memorial that's built after a temple of Zeus, but's got a statue of Lincoln instead."],[339.87,"But also George Washington slept here."],[342.77,"Unlike every house in Virginia and like the rest of New England."],[349.67,"Um, you know, th this battle was fought here."],[353.45,"This, and it's it's soldiers who died in war instead of martyrs."],[360.04,"Those things that were attached to in traditional Christianity to these religious realities, which then some forms at least of Protestantism found problematic, get diverted to political realities."],[377.44,"It's not sort of a recent coincidental accident that certain evangelical churches in the United States have American flags and stuff."],[387.86,"Like this is part of a development that happens surrounding the Second Great Awakening."],[393.56,"Where those those instincts, those things that are proper to Christianity, but had been, even though they'd been displaced."],[401.98,"Sort of, well, no, we don't believe we don't believe in saints anymore."],[406.34,"But we're gonna paint a big painting of George Washington mounting up to heaven and becoming a god in the Capitol Rotunda."],[414.9,"But we would never do that with like St."],[417.06,"Augustine, who we were just mentioning."],[421.22,"Or St. Gregory the Theologian."],[423.46,"Thomas Jefferson, sure."],[425.3,"But not. A saint."],[429.48,"Yeah. But so that gets that gets sort of shifted and it gets smuggled back in."],[434.89,"This is a thing that happens."],[436.27,"Because people and Christian people are Christian people."],[440.75,"And so if you take away confession, you're gonna end up with pastoral psychotherapy."],[447.65,"You're gonna end up with some other thing, generally quote unquote secular, to try to meet that need because it's a need Christians have to talk with someone in pastoral authority about the sin in their life and their struggles."],[465.05,"So if you take the sacramental way of doing that away, it comes back in some other form."],[471.85,"And so this was a in the early 19th century a political form."],[475.13,"It didn't just happen here, it happened in Europe too."],[478.01,"Look at the iconography of like the French Revolution and Napoleon and stuff."],[481.93,"It's very obviously like Roman Catholic iconography flipped political."],[488.19,"So that it's not just a US thing."],[490.11,"This happens in the early 19th century all over the place."],[495.43,"But yeah, so these things, the reason they're they're they're built into humanity."],[502.25,"That's why they were reflected even here in the book of Genesis, you know, 4,000 years ago."],[506.73,"Because humans were the same and God is the same."],[511.45,"Is there some connection also to um the way the the uh the body as in a physical human body um began to be disconnected with certain um views?"],[528.25,"I forget what is a something."],[531.21,"But but that would seem to be not acknowledging a sacred space would seem to be the next step if you separate your own body from being the whole area of the soul."],[539.45,"Yeah The more the more you you lose a sacramental sense of religion, the more iconoclastic your your religion becomes."],[558.65,"Then you're you're you're automatically dissociating religion and the spiritual from the material."],[567.77,"What do you define that iconoclast?"],[571.69,"Uh meaning um, well, literally means smashers of icons, but that's the uh the idea that the holy or the sacred cannot or or should should and not be represented artistically."],[588.31,"Be represented in a physical, material way."],[592.57,"So there are extremes of this."],[594.03,"I I have Presbyterian friends who don't think you should have a cross, just a bare cross in a church, because it will become an idol."],[603.71,"It should just be white walls, because you should just be focusing your mind on the sermon that you're hearing."],[612.27,"And that's a very immaterial mode of religion."],[618.69,"It's very disassociated from the physical and the material and the tactile."],[625.65,"Yeah. Yeah. But here. from the very beginning, God appears to Abram"],[631.49,"Abram sees him with his eyes."],[634.95,"And we're going to see touches, eats with, etc. etc."],[638.08,"Very material. Very these places become very significant and important."],[644.6,"So there's a sense of time, place, space."],[647.77,"And materiality to it."],[649.37,"It's not disassociated from the uh the material world in any way."],[658.81,"And even as we said, calling upon the name of the Lord, worship is taking an animal."],[665.74,"Killing it, because you have to do that before you eat it, but cleaning it."],[669.56,"Taking a portion and offering that portion to God, eating the rest with your family."],[675.42,"You're sharing a meal with God."],[676.86,"This is a material tag."],[678.7,"This is what worship is."],[681.68,"Is this very material, very real participatory experience."],[686.57,"Here from the very beginning."],[690.41,"So chapter 14, verse 1."],[693.61,"Now it came to pass in the reign of Amraphel, king of Shinar, Ariak, king of Eliezer, Keterleomer, king of Elam, and Tidal, king of nations."],[708.25,"So we've got four kings there."],[710.81,"Keterleomer has the best name"],[711.19,"These are different cities."],[716.71,"And that's because every time we have a feast of the holy fathers, and in a minute we'll get into why."],[722.23,"Every time we have the feast of the holy fathers, we read this at Vespers as an Old Testament reading."],[726.63,"And some poor soul who got picked to read this Old Testament reading has to figure out how to pronounce Keterleomer."],[735.75,"So we've got these four kings."],[739.75,"Verse 2 that they made war with Bera, king of Sodom, Bershe, king of Gomorrah, Shinab, king of Admah, Shemeber, king of Zeboyim, and the king of Bela, that is Zoar, who doesn't't get named, because apparently he's, I don know, even lamer than the other ones."],[760.03,"Junior partner. So it's four on five."],[764.99,"So these four northern cities that are further up north in the Jordan Valley decide they're gonna come and attack these five cities that are down in the southern part of the Jordan Valley, near what's now the Dead Sea."],[785.23,"It's kind of implied in the text."],[786.75,"We'll talk about this more when we get to the story of Sodom and Gomorrah, that the Dead Sea isn't necessarily the Dead Sea yet."],[793.55,"But we'll get to that."],[795.67,"Um that's kind of what's being alluded to when it said this is before Sodom and Gomorrah were destroyed."],[802.07,"But we'll get into that more in a minute."],[803.75,"But for right now, kings up north decide we're gonna go and attack those kings down south and pillage them right good."],[812.65,"So they're not trying to come and take over."],[815.61,"Because that would just cause management problems."],[817.87,"They're each the king of a city."],[819.39,"That's about what they can handle."],[820.83,"They're not building an empire here under one emperor but they're conducting a raid."],[826.59,"They're basically robbing pillaging taking slaves right from the Southern kings is their goal."],[834.83,"All of these joined together in the valley of Sidi that is the Salt Sea."],[841.15,"So the Dead, what is now the Dead Sea Valley."],[850.07,"Twelve years they served Ketaleomer, and in the 13th year they rebelled."],[859.43,"So this is saying why they decided to go down there and rampage, is that they had a deal where they were paying tribute to Ketaleomer and the others."],[878.27,"So this is how, this is actually how originally these empires, we talked about with Ur and it being the Ur III period, even though there wasn't an Ur 2 period."],[888.79,"That what that meant when we're talking about quote unquote dynasties is not a succession of kings who are genetically related to each other, but the ability of one king to project his will over neighboring cities."],[901.95,"And this is really what that constituted."],[904.51,"It constituted, hey, you got a nice city here, be a shame if anything happened to it."],[910.35,"And if you send me some tribute once a year in a certain amount, send me some gold, send me some slaves, send me some livestock, then you know we'll make sure nothing bad happens to you."],[922.11,"Heavily implied, I'm the one who's gonna do something bad to you."],[926.17,"But it did carry with it, it did carry with it these agreements."],[931.29,"It did carry with it the idea with a suzerain and a vassal that. if someone else attacks you, I'll defend you"],[940.93,"So, you know, just like the mob in one of those neighborhoods."],[945.95,"You paying the mob, giving them their cut of your business would keep the mob from doing anything to you."],[951.79,"But if somebody else tried to move in on the mob's territory, the mob would take care of them."],[956.61,"So you also didn't have to worry about anybody else."],[959.49,"Same kind of thing here, basically, with these cities."],[962.95,"So they had this agreement, Ketereralaleom and the other kings of the north with the kings of the south."],[968.63,"Um, for 12 years, everything went fine."],[973.03,"You're 13, they didn't pay the tribute."],[978.88,"We don't know exactly why."],[980.64,"Maybe they thought, maybe they were reading the winds, they were like, hey, I don't think they can mount an attack right now."],[986.96,"Maybe they thought, hey, we could fight them off if we have to, let's get our own thing going."],[993.12,"We're not told exactly, but 13th year they don't."],[996.64,"They don't pay up."],[998.16,"So now in the 14th year, Keterlaomer and the kings with him came and cut down the giants in Ashrod Karnaim and strong nations with them, the Zuzim and Ham, the Emim in Shava Kiryatayam."],[1013.8,"im, Sa sayy that five times fast."],[1016.64,"And the Horites in the mountains of Ser, as far as El Paran, which is by the wilderness."],[1025.96,"So these northern kings come and while they're on their way, to go get their tribute, they decide to take out a bunch of giants."],[1037.89,"That's literally what it says."],[1039.17,"That's a direct translation of the Greek."],[1041.09,"It's Yantes. Giants."],[1042.87,"That's where we get the English word giant."],[1045.11,"Um and uh these this is an early appearance."],[1049.83,"So we had, we talked all about back at the beginning of Genesis 6, the Nephilim and the Giants."],[1055.37,"So now we've got, we talked about Nimrod and that kind of recurrence of that phenomenon leading up to the Tower of Babel."],[1064.57,"This is our first appearance of sort of the giant clans."],[1065.93,"These clans that are called giant clans"],[1071.81,"These are going to be a problem going all the way into the book of Joshua."],[1075.81,"In fact, a lot of what's going on in the book of Joshua is going to be dealing with these clans."],[1081.65,"So what makes these clans giant clans?"],[1084.37,"I know people are going to get excited because I'm talking about giants."],[1088.61,"But uh so what makes these clans giant clans, as I've said many times by now, but hey, it always bears repeating."],[1096.91,"Giant does not just refer to someone who's really tall."],[1101.25,"Both Nephilim, which is the Aramaic word for giant, and Yantes could also mean a thug, a bully, a tyrant."],[1109.33,"a And it's sort of like the way we use the phrase strongman in English to refer to dictator."],[1117.45,"Like Panamania. This is dating me, I know."],[1120.25,"I'm looking at people who probably weren't around when we invaded Panama."],[1123.69,"But Panamanian strongman, Manuel Noriega, who we were told about all the time."],[1128.65,"Panamanian strongman, it wasn't this guy has an amazing bench."],[1132.74,"This guy's really ripped."],[1134.26,"It was he's a dictator."],[1136.44,"That was a way of saying he's a dictator."],[1138.12,"It's a way of talking about his evil."],[1141.74,"And so what we know about these giant clans is basically that they were doing what those giants did back in the time of Noah. back in the time of Nimrod"],[1154.98,"Meaning what made you a member of a tribe or a clan in the ancient world was not your genetics."],[1162.1,"You think very much in terms of genetics and ethnicity."],[1165.94,"This is gonna be super important when we get into Exodus."],[1169.12,"Of course, they didn't know DNA existed."],[1172.61,"Um, so we're no DNA tests to see, you know, who you were descended from."],[1177.89,"Uh, and it wasn't based on skin color, and it wasn't based on any other phenotype stuff."],[1183.89,"They weren't measuring people's skulls."],[1186.29,"Um this is it was based on you were a member of a clan, a tribe, a people group, that was all based on ritual."],[1195.65,"That was based on the ritual life of the group."],[1199.21,"So, biblical example later in the Torah, what makes you an Israelite?"],[1205.45,"If you're male, you're circumcised."],[1208.25,"If you're female, you're the wife or daughter of a circumcised male, and you eat the Passover."],[1215.69,"That's what makes you an Israelite."],[1217.69,"Participation in those two rituals."],[1220.57,"That's sort of your bare minimum."],[1222.09,"Obviously, then there's a whole ritual life that is going to be unfolded as we go through the Torah of everything that that involves in terms of dress and eating and all of those things, all those cultural things."],[1232.85,"But you have rituals of initiation, like circumcision, like baptism, for us."],[1240.55,"And you have rituals that perpetuate the unity of the clan, tribe, group, society."],[1250.67,"In the ancient world, these are all focused around sacrifice, because these are all focused on sharing a meal with each other and with the god that you worship, which is primarily what sacrifice is."],[1265.39,"That's what maintains it."],[1266.59,"So that's what makes you part of this group."],[1268.83,"So what makes a Horite a Horite, since that's one of the giant clans mentioned here."],[1274.41,"Is that they're participating in a particular kind of ritual life that's going on with the Horites."],[1281.69,"And we know that this involved two things that made it an abomination."],[1290.21,"Because God is going to point these things out very specifically."],[1293.97,"The book of wisdom, when we get there in a few years, in the Old Testament, is going to name these explicitly when talking about the giant clans."],[1302.77,"These are human sacrifice, which, since sacrifices are meals, involves cannibalism of one kind or another."],[1312.53,"And this is sexualized rituals."],[1316.77,"It involves sexual immorality and demonic possession."],[1321.49,"Participating in those things is what makes you a Horite."],[1327.41,"So there are no innocent Horites."],[1330.37,"They would have to leave that tribe and go join another one"],[1330.89,"Because if they're not participating in those things, they're not a whorite."],[1337.21,"And if they do that, they're not a whorite."],[1342.89,"So this is what constitutes these groups, are these demonic rituals."],[1353.13,"And so again, we're seeing a pattern here."],[1355.87,"That's telegraphing things that are going to come later in the Torah and later in Joshua and even into the story of David."],[1364.43,"He fights more giants than just the one."],[1366.91,"And that is that God is going to say, these groups have to be wiped out"],[1372.4,"Does that mean you need to kill every single one of them?"],[1376.12,"No. Because if a person goes and becomes a member of a different group, they're not a Horite anymore."],[1384.6,"They're not a Gergazite anymore."],[1386.76,"They're not an Amalekite anymore."],[1391.64,"So when God says there cannot be any more Amalekites, he means the Amalekite way of life."],[1399.56,"The demonic ritual way of life that constitutes the Amalekites as a people."],[1404.04,"That has to be put to an end."],[1409.96,"Does it require that every single one of them die?"],[1414.28,"We're going to see it does require that any unrepentant ones do."],[1419.08,"Any who refuse to cease participating in those acts do"],[1426.96,"If the only way we can stop you from sacrificing humans and engaging in cannibalism is to kill you, then that's what we have to do."],[1435.6,"That's where the Torah is gonna go kind of unapologetically."],[1440.96,"If that's the only way we can stop you, then that's what we have to do."],[1445.2,"And while people love to get up in arms and be deeply upset about the idea that, you know, a kid might have been killed in Palestine by Joshua, you know, 3200 years ago."],[1464.24,"First of all, I'd point out they're not all that upset about uh children getting killed in Palestine today."],[1469.56,"But also, I would submit to you that any one of us, if we knew our neighbor, was murdering people."],[1479.56,"If our we knew our neighbor was Jeffrey Dahmer, for sure, no doubt, this is what he is doing."],[1487.0,"We would not sit there and pray that he would repent."],[1492.36,"We would call a person with a gun, to go to that house and stop him from doing that."],[1500.33,"And if in the process of that person with a gun trying to stop him, our Jeffrey Dahmer neighbor ended up getting shot."],[1508.73,"We might be motivated by Christian piety to pray for his soul"],[1515.27,"But if that's what had to happen to stop him from doing that, then that's what's presented here."],[1525.51,"And so why."],[1527.41,"Who cares that these northern kings, like Keterleomer, uh went and killed a bunch of giants on their way to go get their tribute from these other cities?"],[1538.29,"Well, this is Genesis telling us who the good guys and the bad guys are in this fight that's about to happen."],[1547.62,"Who's the good guys?"],[1548.66,"Who's the bad guys?"],[1549.46,"We've already been told the men of Sodom were exceedingly wicked."],[1551.7,"So that's already kind of telegraphing to us who the bad guys are"],[1554.88,"The king of Sodom, the king of Gomorrah being on the one side."],[1558.32,"But now we see, on the other side, that these guys, whether purposely or indirectly, are sort of doing God's will."],[1568.83,"As it's going to be laid out later in Deuteronomy."],[1571.07,"Deuteronomy 2 is actually going to be talking about God driving out these giant clans before all kinds of groups."],[1579.23,"Edomites, Ammonites. Descendants, descendants of Abraham."],[1583.94,"So that's why this is included."],[1586.44,"To give us a sense."],[1589.25,"These aren't the bad guys."],[1590.29,"Because there's going to be some conflict between them and Abram here, but. getting ahead of myself, yeah"],[1598.01,"Well, that's kind of like the way the Edomites were too."],[1601.69,"Like they uh there wasn't always like 100% peace between them in Israel."],[1607.03,"Well, one of the groups in in Deuteronomy 2 is the Philistines who drive out some of the giants in Deuteronomy 2."],[1614.55,"So yeah, this isn't necessarily a full-on endorsement, of that people group."],[1620.41,"But like Edom's related to Israel."],[1623.85,"like Like are these are we supposed to make the assumption that these northern kingdoms are somehow like connected to Abraham and sort of a similar way?"],[1634.33,"Well, we're gonna see there's conflict, but this is telegraphing something. even bigger in the history of Israel"],[1640.93,"Because even if we don't want to say they're really good, good guys."],[1647.43,"They're kind of doing God's will."],[1649.43,"And they're coming from the north and they're disciplining these wicked people in the south."],[1654.23,"Kind of like a foretaste of Babylon or that's what's going to happen in the exile eventually."],[1660.63,"Well, both ended the destruction of Israel, really."],[1663.97,"Israel's gonna get invaded from the north by pagans, but those pagans are gonna be doing God's will to discipline Israel for their sins."],[1674.05,"So this is another one of those broad outline patterns again that we talked about last time."],[1679.18,"The beginning of a pattern that we're going to see play out."],[1682.18,"So is this is this like the is this like when Joshua, it's like I always thought it was strange they had said like the uh hornets or something went before them?"],[1691.06,"Yeah. And so is this just like this kind of um God's will is gonna be done either through his creation or through."],[1701.19,"There's all these different manners, I guess, in touch with that."],[1704.25,"Like the plagues, God gets in locusts."],[1706.19,"God gets in, God gets it."],[1707.71,"God can discipline many different ways."],[1710.21,"And sometimes what he sends is an invading army."],[1714.77,"That includes Joshua's army."],[1718.74,"Coming to Canaan to take out the giant clans, that also includes Assyria coming to take out the ten northern tribes and Babylon coming to take Judah into exile as a result of their sins."],[1733.34,"Pagans invading from the north cause the like symbolic idea of demons are coming from the north as well."],[1740.3,"It's related. It's related."],[1742.86,"So that's part of it."],[1744.14,"Invasions come from the north."],[1746.92,"The other big piece, though, is going to be particular things in the northern part of the land of Israel, surrounding Dan and Mount Hermon and those kind of things that are in the north of the piece of land."],[1759.8,"But yes, it is also where attack and invasion comes from."],[1763.88,"And you're also the reason why they have to come on the birth."],[1767.68,"And the north is all the left too."],[1770.64,"Yes. Yeah, if you're facing east."],[1774.1,"And yeah, I didn't mention that when Abram was saying if you go right, I'll go left."],[1777.78,"If you go left, I'll go right."],[1779.14,"That wasn't just a vague reference."],[1781.54,"That's actually in the original, like, sort of, if you go north, I'll go south."],[1785.38,"If you go south, saying we'll go in opposite directions."],[1788.04,"Yeah. Yeah. So yes, they're coming from the north to discipline."],[1795.34,"Wicked kings. God's will is being done, even if they're not totally right."],[1802.54,"In the right."],[1806.86,"Okay, verse 7."],[1808.06,"Then they turned back and came to. En Mishpat, that is Kadesh"],[1814.1,"That's the place of justice, that is Kadesh."],[1817.02,"Um, and cut down all the princes of the Amalekites and also the Amorites who dwelt in Hazazan Tamar."],[1828.54,"Now, one of the most interesting things about this is that you notice I just mentioned the Amalekites."],[1836.86,"Amalek is a descendant of Esau, according to the genealogies, we're going to read in a few chapters."],[1845.26,"So, this is another place where names are being projected into the past."],[1850.26,"Number one, and number two, where tribal groups again are being identified by their ritual life, by their cultural life."],[1859.88,"Not by ethnicity or biological descent."],[1865.61,"So even though the Amalekites are going to end up named after this descendant of Esau, they're going to be practicing this way of life that pre-existed."],[1874.43,"That guy who they came to be named after."],[1878.57,"And we're going to see when we get to that genealogy, we're tipped off there too, about Amalek, right in the genealogy."],[1884.97,"But Verse 8."],[1889.77,"Now the king of Sodom, the king of Gomorrah, the king of Adma, the king of Zeboyim, and the King of Bela, that is Zoar, just in case you forgot the list before, went out and joined together in battle in the Valley of Sidim against Keralaomer, king of Edom, Elam, Tidal, King of Nations, Amraphel, King of Shinar, and Ariak, king of El Asar."],[1913.25,"Four kings against five."],[1915.17,"Just in case you forgot from a few verses ago."],[1921.81,"Now the valley of Siddh was full of asphalt pits, tar pits, basically."],[1927.89,"And the kings of Sodom and Gomorrah fled and fell in there, but those left fled to the mountains."],[1933.81,"Okay, so the five kings from the south lose."],[1937.89,"When they try to flee, they get stuck."],[1940.17,"Their armies get routed pretty thoroughly."],[1942.37,"They go and hide"],[1942.73,"Uh the ones who do manage to get away flee off into the mountains."],[1945.45,"They don't even go back to the cities."],[1949.66,"Then they took all the cavalry of Sodom and Gomorrah and all their provisions and went their way."],[1954.62,"They also took Lot, Abram's brother's son, who dwelt in Sodom and his goods, and departed."],[1960.3,"So now the army is scattered."],[1962.3,"Time to get that tribute that they didn't want to pay us."],[1965.72,"So they go and seize all the wealth from the cities, pillage everything, take the horses."],[1972.14,"Take the, and take a whole ton of the city folk as slaves."],[1977.5,"And Lot is one of them."],[1980.51,"Now, if they're pillaging the cities."],[1983.15,"Lots Lot's starting to become part of the local culture, shall we say?"],[1992.14,"He's starting to become one of the people of Sodom."],[1999.42,"This is progressing in a bad way."],[2001.6,"But so they all get rounded up."],[2003.52,"They all get taken."],[2004.64,"Lot included gets taken as a slave."],[2008.0,"Verse 13. Then one who escaped came and told Abram the Hebrew, who at that time dwelt by the Oak of Mamre, the Amorite, brother of Eshkol, and brother of Aner, and they were allies with Abram."],[2023.8,"So Mamre is around at the time."],[2027.76,"around at the time. It was still gonna be named after him much later, we know, but he's"],[2034.17,"And he and his fellows and his family, they have an agreement."],[2038.83,"So Abram's not just like squatting on his land and grazing it."],[2042.13,"They have a they have an agreement with each other."],[2045.33,"Now, you may notice here he's referred to as an Amorite."],[2049.41,"Giant fans may know Amorites are generally a giant clan."],[2053.73,"The word Amorite is used in different ways."],[2057.9,"Uh Amorite technically comes from a Muru and just means westerner."],[2064.38,"So any person from northern Palestine, what's now Lebanon, uh Western Syria, would have been called an Amorite."],[2072.46,"Being from that region made you an Amorite"],[2077.68,"So Amorite is not always referring to a specific people group."],[2084.13,"I know that's confusing because So would that be like saying you're European, but like there are different cultures, either good or bad, within that very broad category?"],[2096.37,"Yeah. Um. Or even less of an implication than that."],[2101.81,"Yeah, I mean it it''ss kind kind of of like like um calling someone Cajun."],[2111.19,"Because there're just like a lot of people."],[2112.39,"Yeah, or or calling calling calling a Canadian an American."],[2115.75,"Because they live in North America"],[2117.15,"Okay. Like, versus calling them a Canadian."],[2126.75,"So American is used to mean citizen of the United States."],[2132.29,"But technically it could also be used for anyone who lives on the continent."],[2138.26,"Um that's not quite it, but closer."],[2145.78,"Yeah. Um, yeah."],[2148.18,"So it can just mean you're from this region."],[2150.42,"That work could both mean you're from this region or you're part of this particular people group."],[2156.36,"That is originally from that region."],[2160.49,"Um it might be like calling calling a uh a Cajun person French."],[2172.83,"So a French person could be a person from France."],[2178.13,"It could also mean a French speaker."],[2180.79,"Someone who's from a French culture, you know, someone Quebecois could be called French, French Canadian."],[2189.0,"Um sort of like that."],[2191.64,"So this is not saying at any rate that Mamry is a giant."],[2195.53,"It's not trying to associate him in that way, it's just he's he's that's where from."],[2200.61,"Um we'll have a similar conversation later on in the Old Testament about the word Hittite, because that is another word that gets used in two different ways."],[2210.37,"Um Kushite gets used that way too."],[2213.89,"So there's an actual kingdom in Africa called Kush."],[2217.01,"And the people there are Cushites, but the Egyptians also called anyone from south of them a Cushite."],[2229.01,"So Ethiopians they called Cushites, people from the Sudan area they called Cushites, people from the southern part of Libya they called Cushites."],[2239.01,"Yeah. Usually uh most rudible."],[2243.45,"Like uh I think uh the Apaches did the same thing, it's like they were the people not everybody else."],[2250.58,"Yeah, yeah. But so Cushite can mean an actual person from Cush, or it could just mean someone from the area that area around Egypt."],[2260.58,"So same kind of thing with Amorite."],[2262.1,"It can mean someone from that area in Western Syria, or it can mean an actual Amorites."],[2274.62,"Now when Abram heard his brother Lot was taken captive, he armed his 318 trained servants, born in his own house, and went in pursuit as far as Dan."],[2286.74,"Here's another one of those city names."],[2288.88,"Because Dan, of course, is named after his great grandson Dan."],[2295.25,"And not by his great grandson Dan, by the tribe of Dan, when they come and take it, and it's explicitly called Laish before that."],[2305.01,"Judges is going to tell us that."],[2328.05,"Even Joshua. But so it takes his 318 trained servants."],[2338.91,"People didn't have standing armies."],[2340.36,"So he rounds up his 318 military-age men."],[2346.36,"They're probably using farm implements, they're using clubs and sickles and that kind of thing."],[2350.52,"They don't have armor, they don't have weapons per se."],[2352.78,"They're using those kinds of things as weapons."],[2355.02,"So this is why this gets read."],[2362.7,"Never mind. Put a pin in that."],[2364.54,"Because we only got a couple verses left."],[2366.7,"Verse 15. So he divided his horses against them by night, and he had his servants attacked and pursued them as far as Hoba, north of Damascus."],[2375.86,"So he brought back all the cavalry of Sodom and also brought back his brother Lot and his goods, as well as the women in the people."],[2384.18,"So Abram goes up there, he waits until after dark, he takes his 318 men, he splits them up, they do a pincer move."],[2392.95,"They come in and they rescue the slaves of the stuff that they took from Sodom and get away."],[2400.68,"Does his rescue operation."],[2405.0,"So, yeah, so a lot of people will tell you that the whole reason that this passage that we just read gets read every time we have a feast of the Holy Fathers."],[2421.04,"And by the Holy Fathers, I mean usually the Holy Fathers of an ecumenical council."],[2424.8,"There's a few other places too, but anytime, like First Ecumenical Council, Sunday of the Tribal Orthodoxy, Seventh Ecumenical Councils, the Seven Ecumenical Councils, the first four ecumenical councils."],[2436.72,"Night before at Vespers, we read this."],[2438.96,"They'll say, Well, at the Council of Nicaea, there were 318 bishops."],[2445.6,"And see, Abram has 318 men."],[2453.12,"And sometimes they'll do it even wonkier."],[2456.8,"Yeah. And sometimes they'll do an even wonkier version of that and say, ah, this passage prophesied the council of Nicaea."],[2464.32,"Um, that's right"],[2466.68,"In which case you're just being wacky, man."],[2472.04,"This is very surface level."],[2473.8,"This is very surface level."],[2474.92,"Like the 318 number is interesting."],[2477.69,"There's an interesting detail."],[2479.5,"But that's not really what's going on here."],[2482.06,"Because what's the story about here in Genesis?"],[2486.7,"The story is about."],[2489.07,"Lot wandered off."],[2492.27,"He got enraptured by the world, by the wisdom of the world, the civilization, the culture."],[2500.11,"He got drawn in."],[2501.55,"He got taken captive."],[2504.99,"He got enslaved."],[2508.37,"And Abram, his father, the patriarch, the head, who has this position from God, went and found him, defeated those who had enslaved him, sets him free, and rescues him."],[2526.87,"So the reason we read this before one of those feasts, one of the councils, the fathers of the councils, is that this is the church's understanding of what's happening at these councils."],[2540.39,"So using the Council of Nicaea as an example, well beyond the number 318."],[2547.05,"What happened? Arius."],[2551.94,"Learned scholarly guy from Alexandria, the center of learning and scholarship for the whole world."],[2559.7,"Comes up with his teaching, starts drawing people off from the church to become his followers."],[2566.04,"Taking them captive with his doctrine, with his ideas."],[2572.17,"Causing them to become enslaved."],[2576.65,"The fathers of the church who have that position from God are coming in the council to rescue the church, to set the church free from this threat."],[2592.89,"That's how the church sees the councils."],[2594.57,"It's not just, oh, hey, look, 318"],[2594.93,"That's what the church is communicating to us when we read this story."],[2601.81,"See, the council of Nicaea is from God."],[2604.74,"But this is the church's self understanding of what's going on there."],[2610.02,"This isn't about fine points of theology."],[2613.7,"This isn't about imperial power."],[2615.38,"This isn't about any of those things in the church's self understanding."],[2619.7,"This is about the fact that we, as unflattering as it is, are sheep, and we have shepherds."],[2629.22,"I have a bishop"],[2629.82,"That includes me."],[2630.62,"I have a shepherd."],[2634.1,"Everybody has. Shepherds."],[2636.82,"So the shepherds have a chief shepherd."],[2641.26,"Who are trusted with that position?"],[2643.82,"We like sheep tend to wander astray and get into trouble."],[2649.31,"Go the wrong way."],[2651.87,"Sheep will wander into a stream, and their wool will start absorbing water and they will drown standing there because they're too dumb to walk out of the stream."],[2662.19,"That's why I say it's kind of unflattering that we get compared to sheep a lot."],[2667.15,"But if you think about how sin works in our lives, we kind of do that too."],[2671.72,"Because we're too dumb to abandon it."],[2674.1,"We just stand there and drown."],[2677.26,"Um, and so there are shepherds to come reporting with God to rescue us from ourselves."],[2684.8,"And to set us free from that and deliver us from that."],[2690.16,"And so when church discipline happens, when correction happens right from above from our shepherds to put us back in line, this is also how we should see it."],[2703.04,"We shouldn't see this as an attack on us at least."],[2708.85,"We should see this as concern, as rescue, as being set free from what we've allowed to take us captive."],[2720.29,"That's the goal."],[2723.49,"It's for our for our our benefit."],[2727.93,"So I think, even though we're not at the end of a chapter, that we will stop here because we're going to get into Belchizedek, and that I have a feeling might be a long discussion."],[2741.45,"Yeah. So that's we'll go ahead and uh stop here for tonight, and we'll pick up next week in uh verse uh 17 of chapter 14."],[2752.97,"So thank you everybody."],[2756.25,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2763.61,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2768.97,"That's whole counsel at ancientface.com."]],"o":[{"t":22.48,"lv":2,"h":"13:14-18: the promise, altar number three","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.14-15: after <b>Lot separates</b> - \"lift your eyes... <b>north, south, east, west</b>\"; all the land you see I give to <b>you and your seed forever</b></li><li class=\"d0\">v.16: seed as the <b>dust of the earth</b> - \"if a man could number the dust...\"</li><li class=\"d0\">v.17: \"<b>Arise, walk</b> through its length and breadth\"</li><li class=\"d0\">v.18: moves tent to the <b>Oak of Mamre in Hebron</b>; builds an altar - <b>altar number three</b></li><li class=\"d1\">near <b>Hebron</b> = later in <b>Judah's</b> territory</li></ul>"},{"t":71.32,"lv":3,"h":"The Oak of Mamre as a landmark","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Mamre is a person</b>; the oak = already <b>ancient, impressive</b>, on his property, a <b>landmark</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Names that date to <b>later periods</b> keep showing up in Genesis</li><li class=\"d1\">the oak still there when Israelites arrive - <b>post-Joshua</b>, when <b>Caleb</b> came to Hebron</li><li class=\"d1\">pointed out because \"you can still go there and see that oak\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"this is where Abraham stayed, <b>that altar</b>, that spot\"</li></ul>"},{"t":130.29,"lv":2,"h":"Sacred space and pilgrimage","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Beyond single vs. multiple sanctuaries: <b>geographical locales</b> matter</li><li class=\"d0\">Shows something about religion in the Torah</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>references specific places</b> - places in <b>3D space</b> you can go are <b>religiously relevant</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>pilgrimage sites</b>: people go to see this oak, recount what happened to Abram there</li><li class=\"d1\">part of their <b>faith, heritage, inheritance</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Sacred space</b> already in Genesis</li><li class=\"d1\">God = <b>God of Abram</b> → important places in Abram's life become <b>holy to the God of Abram</b>; later <b>Isaac and Jacob</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God identifies himself with Abram → the places become identified as sacred</li><li class=\"d0\">Friends from other Christian traditions <b>dislike sacred space, pilgrimage</b></li><li class=\"d1\">some decry it and <b>go to the Holy Land anyway</b></li><li class=\"d1\">told his parents about the <b>Church of the Holy Sepulchre</b> - they \"didn't believe it was real\" (Southern Baptist) - \"we don't actually know...\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Built into</b> Israelite religion, Judaism, Christianity</li><li class=\"d1\">unnoticed: any <b>historic landmark / battle tour</b> tells the story of the place</li></ul>"},{"t":310.64,"lv":2,"h":"Sacred instincts redirected to politics","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Early 19th century</b>: religious sensibility <b>attached to political realities</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Lincoln Memorial</b> built after a <b>temple of Zeus</b>, statue of Lincoln instead</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>George Washington slept here</b>\" - \"like every house in Virginia\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"this battle was fought here\" - <b>soldiers who died in war instead of martyrs</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Things attached to traditional Christianity, which some Protestantism found problematic → <b>diverted to political realities</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not coincidence that some <b>evangelical churches have American flags</b> - tied to the <b>Second Great Awakening</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"we don't believe in saints\" but paint <b>George Washington ascending to heaven</b> in the <b>Capitol Rotunda</b>; never with <b>St. Augustine</b> or <b>St. Gregory the Theologian</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Smuggled back in</b> - \"Christian people are Christian people\"</li><li class=\"d1\">remove <b>confession</b> → <b>pastoral psychotherapy</b>, something \"secular\" for the <b>need to talk to pastoral authority</b> about sin</li><li class=\"d1\">remove the sacramental way → returns in another form; here <b>political</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not just US: <b>French Revolution, Napoleon</b> - Roman Catholic iconography <b>flipped political</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Built into humanity - in Genesis <b>4,000 years ago</b>: \"humans were the same and God is the same\"</li></ul>"},{"t":511.45,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: body, iconoclasm, material worship","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: connection to views disconnecting the <b>physical body</b>? Not acknowledging sacred space = next step if body separate from the soul</li><li class=\"d0\">Lose a <b>sacramental sense</b> → <b>more iconoclastic</b></li><li class=\"d1\">automatically <b>dissociating the spiritual from the material</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: define <b>iconoclast</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">literally <b>\"smashers of icons\"</b>; holy can't / shouldn't be <b>represented artistically, materially</b></li><li class=\"d1\">extremes: <b>Presbyterian</b> friends - no bare <b>cross</b> in church, it'll become an <b>idol</b>; white walls, focus on the <b>sermon</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"very <b>immaterial</b> mode of religion\" - disassociated from the physical, tactile</li></ul>"},{"t":625.65,"lv":3,"h":"Genesis is material","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">From the very beginning God <b>appears to Abram</b>; Abram <b>sees him with his eyes</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we'll see him <b>touch, eat with</b> him - \"very material\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Places significant: <b>time, place, space, materiality</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Calling on the name of the Lord</b> = worship</li><li class=\"d1\">take an <b>animal</b>, kill it (needed before eating anyway), clean it</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>offer a portion</b> to God, eat the rest with <b>family</b> → <b>sharing a meal with God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"very material, very real <b>participatory</b> experience\" - from the very beginning</li></ul>"},{"t":690.41,"lv":2,"h":"14:1-3: four kings against five","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.1: <b>Amraphel</b> of Shinar, <b>Arioch</b> of Ellasar, <b>Chedorlaomer</b> of Elam, <b>Tidal</b> king of nations - four kings, <b>different cities</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"Chedorlaomer has the best name\"</li><li class=\"d1\">read at <b>Vespers</b> as an OT reading on every <b>feast of the Holy Fathers</b> (why, in a minute) - \"poor soul who has to pronounce Chedorlaomer\"</li><li class=\"d0\">v.2: war with <b>Bera</b> of Sodom, <b>Birsha</b> of Gomorrah, <b>Shinab</b> of Admah, <b>Shemeber</b> of Zeboiim, king of <b>Bela = Zoar</b> (unnamed - \"junior partner\", \"even lamer\")</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Four on five</b></li><li class=\"d1\">four <b>northern</b> cities vs. five in the <b>southern Jordan Valley</b>, near the <b>Dead Sea</b></li><li class=\"d1\">implied: Dead Sea <b>not yet the Dead Sea</b> (before Sodom and Gomorrah destroyed) - later</li><li class=\"d0\">Goal: <b>raid</b>, not empire</li><li class=\"d1\">each king of <b>one city</b> - \"about what they can handle\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>robbing, pillaging, taking slaves</b></li><li class=\"d0\">v.3: join in the <b>Valley of Siddim</b> = the <b>Salt Sea</b></li></ul>"},{"t":850.07,"lv":3,"h":"Tribute and the suzerain-vassal deal","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.4: <b>twelve years</b> they served Chedorlaomer; <b>13th year rebelled</b> → why the raid: <b>tribute</b></li><li class=\"d0\">How early <b>empires</b> worked - cf. <b>Ur III</b> (\"even though there wasn't an Ur II\")</li><li class=\"d1\">\"dynasty\" = not related kings but <b>one king projecting his will over neighboring cities</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"nice city... <b>be a shame if anything happened</b>\"; yearly <b>tribute</b> - gold, slaves, livestock</li><li class=\"d1\">implied: <b>I'm the one who'll do something bad</b></li><li class=\"d0\">But a real <b>agreement</b>: suzerain and <b>vassal</b> - if someone else attacks you, <b>I'll defend you</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like <b>the mob</b>: pay the cut, they leave you alone <b>and keep rivals out</b></li><li class=\"d0\">12 years fine; 13th no tribute</li><li class=\"d1\">not told why: <b>reading the winds</b>? thought they could <b>fight them off</b>?</li></ul>"},{"t":998.16,"lv":2,"h":"14:5-6: the giants cut down","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.5-6: 14th year, Chedorlaomer and kings <b>cut down the giants in Ashteroth Karnaim</b>, the <b>Zuzim</b> in Ham, the <b>Emim</b> in Shaveh Kiriathaim, the <b>Horites</b> in Mount <b>Seir</b> to <b>El Paran</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"say that five times fast\"</li><li class=\"d0\">On the way to collect tribute they \"take out a bunch of <b>giants</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">literally; direct translation of Greek <b><i>gigantes</i></b> → English \"giant\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>First appearance of giant clans</b></li><li class=\"d1\">earlier: <b>Nephilim and giants</b> (Gen. 6); <b>Nimrod</b> as recurrence → Babel</li><li class=\"d1\">a problem <b>all the way into Joshua</b> - much of Joshua = dealing with these clans</li></ul>"},{"t":1081.65,"lv":2,"h":"What makes a giant clan","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Giant\" doesn't just mean <b>tall</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Nephilim</b> (Aramaic) and <i>gigantes</i> can mean <b>thug, bully, tyrant</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like <b>\"strongman\"</b> for dictator: <b>Manuel Noriega</b>, \"Panamanian strongman\" - not \"amazing bench\", but <b>dictator</b>, his evil</li><li class=\"d0\">They did what the giants did <b>in Noah's time, Nimrod's time</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Clan membership <b>not genetics</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we think in <b>genetics, ethnicity</b> (important for Exodus); no DNA tests, <b>skin color, skull measuring</b></li><li class=\"d1\">based on <b>ritual life</b> of the group</li><li class=\"d0\">Example: what makes you an <b>Israelite</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">male: <b>circumcised</b>; female: wife/daughter of a circumcised male; <b>eat the Passover</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the bare minimum; then a whole ritual life - dress, eating, culture</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Initiation rituals</b> (circumcision; <b>baptism</b> for us) + rituals perpetuating <b>unity</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ancient world: around <b>sacrifice</b> = <b>sharing a meal</b> with each other and the god</li></ul>"},{"t":1268.83,"lv":3,"h":"The Horites' ritual life","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">What makes a <b>Horite</b> a Horite = participating in a particular <b>ritual life</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Two <b>abominations</b> - named by God; <b>Book of Wisdom</b> names them for giant clans</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>human sacrifice</b> - sacrifices are meals → <b>cannibalism</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>sexualized rituals</b>: sexual immorality, <b>demonic possession</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>No innocent Horites</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not participating → not a Horite; must <b>leave and join another tribe</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Groups <b>constituted by demonic rituals</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1353.13,"lv":2,"h":"Why these clans must end","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Pattern <b>telegraphing</b> the Torah, Joshua, even <b>David</b> (fights more giants than one)</li><li class=\"d1\">God: these groups <b>have to be wiped out</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Does it mean killing <b>every single one</b>? <b>No</b></li><li class=\"d1\">join another group → <b>not a Horite, Girgashite, Amalekite</b> anymore</li><li class=\"d1\">\"no more Amalekites\" = end the <b>Amalekite way of life</b>, the <b>demonic ritual life</b></li><li class=\"d0\">But <b>unrepentant ones</b> - those refusing to stop - <b>do</b> have to die</li><li class=\"d1\">\"if the only way to stop you sacrificing humans and cannibalism is to kill you, then that's what we have to do\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Torah goes there <b>unapologetically</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1445.2,"lv":3,"h":"The Jeffrey Dahmer test","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">People upset about a child killed in Palestine by <b>Joshua 3,200 years ago</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"not all that upset about children killed in Palestine <b>today</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Neighbor is <b>Jeffrey Dahmer</b>, known for sure</li><li class=\"d1\">we wouldn't just pray he'd repent - <b>call a person with a gun</b></li><li class=\"d1\">if he gets shot stopping him: pray for his soul out of Christian piety, but <b>that's what had to happen</b></li><li class=\"d0\">That's what's presented here</li></ul>"},{"t":1525.51,"lv":2,"h":"Why Genesis tells us this: good guys and bad guys","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Who cares that northern kings <b>killed giants</b> on the way?</li><li class=\"d0\">Genesis tells us <b>who's good and who's bad</b> in the coming fight</li><li class=\"d1\">men of Sodom <b>exceedingly wicked</b> - king of Sodom, Gomorrah on one side</li><li class=\"d1\">other side: these kings, <b>purposely or indirectly, doing God's will</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Deuteronomy 2</b>: God driving out giant clans before groups - <b>Edomites, Ammonites</b>, descendants of Abraham</li><li class=\"d0\">\"These aren't the bad guys\" - some conflict with Abram, but \"getting ahead of myself\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1598.01,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: \"not a full endorsement\" and the north","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q/comment: like the <b>Edomites</b> - not always 100% peace with Israel</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Deut. 2</b> also names the <b>Philistines</b> driving out giants</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not necessarily a <b>full-on endorsement</b> of that people group\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Edom related to Israel; Q: northern kings connected to Abraham? → there'll be <b>conflict</b>, but \"telegraphing something bigger\"</li><li class=\"d0\">They're <b>kind of doing God's will</b></li><li class=\"d1\">from the <b>north</b>, <b>disciplining the wicked south</b></li><li class=\"d1\">foretaste of <b>Babylon</b> / the <b>exile</b>; Israel invaded from the north by pagans who discipline Israel for <b>sins</b></li><li class=\"d1\">another <b>broad outline pattern</b>, as last time</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: Joshua - <b>hornets going before them</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">God's will through creation: <b>plagues, locusts</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sometimes an <b>invading army</b>: <b>Joshua's army</b> vs. giant clans; <b>Assyria</b> vs. ten northern tribes; <b>Babylon</b> taking Judah</li><li class=\"d0\">Comment: pagans from the north - <b>demons coming from the north</b> too</li><li class=\"d1\">related; plus things in northern Israel: <b>Dan, Mount Hermon</b></li><li class=\"d1\">north = <b>left</b>, facing east</li><li class=\"d0\">Also: Abram's \"<b>if you go right I'll go left</b>\" wasn't vague - original: <b>north / south</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So they come from the north to <b>discipline wicked kings</b> - God's will done \"even if they're not totally right\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1806.86,"lv":2,"h":"14:7: Kadesh and the Amalekites","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.7: turn back to <b>En Mishpat = Kadesh</b> (\"place of justice\"); cut down all the <b>princes of the Amalekites</b> and the <b>Amorites</b> in <b>Hazazon Tamar</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Amalek</b> = descendant of <b>Esau</b> (genealogies in a few chapters)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>names projected into the past</b> again</li><li class=\"d1\">tribal groups identified by <b>ritual and cultural life</b>, <b>not ethnicity or descent</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Amalekites named after Esau's descendant but practicing a way of life <b>that pre-existed</b> him</li><li class=\"d1\">genealogy will <b>tip us off</b> about Amalek</li></ul>"},{"t":1884.97,"lv":2,"h":"14:8-12: the battle in the Valley of Siddim","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.8-9: the <b>five kings</b> go out, join battle in <b>Siddim</b> against Chedorlaomer, Tidal, Amraphel, Arioch - \"four kings against five\"</li><li class=\"d0\">v.10: valley <b>full of asphalt (tar) pits</b></li><li class=\"d1\">kings of Sodom and Gomorrah <b>flee and fall in</b>; the rest flee to the mountains</li><li class=\"d1\">armies <b>routed</b>; survivors hide in the mountains - not back to the cities</li><li class=\"d0\">v.11-12: take all the <b>goods and provisions</b> of Sodom and Gomorrah; take <b>Lot</b>, Abram's brother's son, who dwelt in <b>Sodom</b>, and his goods</li><li class=\"d1\">\"time to get that tribute they didn't want to pay\"</li><li class=\"d1\">pillage everything, take <b>horses</b>, a ton of <b>city folk as slaves</b> - Lot one of them</li></ul>"},{"t":1980.51,"lv":3,"h":"Lot becoming a Sodomite","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Lot <b>pillaged with the cities</b> - he's <b>part of the local culture</b></li><li class=\"d1\">starting to become <b>one of the people of Sodom</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"progressing in a <b>bad way</b>\"; taken as a <b>slave</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2008.0,"lv":2,"h":"14:13: Mamre the Amorite","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.13: <b>one who escaped</b> tells <b>Abram the Hebrew</b>, by the Oak of <b>Mamre the Amorite</b>, brother of <b>Eshcol</b> and <b>Aner</b> - <b>allies</b> with Abram</li><li class=\"d0\">Mamre around at the time; still named after him much later</li><li class=\"d0\">They have an <b>agreement</b> - Abram not \"squatting on his land and grazing it\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2045.33,"lv":3,"h":"\"Amorite\": a region, not necessarily a giant clan","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Amorites generally = a giant clan, but word used <b>different ways</b></li><li class=\"d1\">from <b>Amurru</b> = <b>westerner</b>; anyone from northern Palestine, <b>Lebanon, Western Syria</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not always a specific people group</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: like saying <b>European</b>? - yes, or less: like calling someone <b>Cajun</b>; a <b>Canadian</b> an <b>American</b> (lives in North America) vs. citizen of the US</li><li class=\"d1\">or calling a Cajun / <b>Quebecois</b> \"<b>French</b>\" - from France, French speaker, French culture</li><li class=\"d0\">So not saying <b>Mamre is a giant</b> - just where he's from</li><li class=\"d0\">Same later: <b>Hittite</b> (two uses); <b>Kushite</b></li><li class=\"d1\">real kingdom of <b>Kush</b> in Africa; Egyptians called <b>anyone south of them</b> Cushite - <b>Ethiopians, Sudan, southern Libya</b></li><li class=\"d1\">cf. Apaches: \"the people, not everybody else\"</li><li class=\"d1\">same with Amorite: from the region <b>or</b> an actual Amorite</li></ul>"},{"t":2274.62,"lv":2,"h":"14:14-16: Abram's rescue","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.14: hears <b>his brother (kinsman) Lot</b> captured → arms <b>318 trained servants born in his house</b>; pursues <b>as far as Dan</b></li><li class=\"d1\">another <b>updated city name</b>: Dan named by the <b>tribe of Dan</b> when they take it; before that <b>Laish</b> (Judges tells us)</li><li class=\"d1\">no standing armies: rounds up <b>318 military-age men</b></li><li class=\"d1\">probably <b>farm implements, clubs, sickles</b> - no armor, \"weapons per se\"</li><li class=\"d0\">v.15: divides at <b>night</b>, attacks, pursues to <b>Hobah, north of Damascus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">v.16: brings back <b>all the cavalry of Sodom</b>, <b>Lot and his goods</b>, the <b>women and the people</b></li><li class=\"d1\">waits until dark, splits them, <b>pincer move</b>, rescue the slaves and goods</li></ul>"},{"t":2405.0,"lv":3,"h":"318 and the Council of Nicaea","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why read at <b>every feast of the Holy Fathers</b> - usually fathers of an <b>ecumenical council</b>; Vespers the night before (First Council, Sunday of Orthodoxy, Seventh, first four councils)</li><li class=\"d0\">Common explanation: <b>318 bishops at Nicaea</b> = Abram's <b>318</b> men</li><li class=\"d1\">\"wonkier\" version: passage <b>prophesied the council of Nicaea</b> - \"you're being wacky\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"very <b>surface level</b>\"; 318 an interesting detail, <b>not what's going on</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2482.06,"lv":2,"h":"The real point: Lot taken, Abram rescues","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Story: <b>Lot wandered off</b></li><li class=\"d1\">enraptured by the <b>world, wisdom of the world, civilization, culture</b>; drawn in, taken <b>captive</b>, <b>enslaved</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Abram</b>, the father, patriarch, with <b>position from God</b>, finds him, defeats captors, <b>sets him free</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Church's understanding of the councils</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Nicaea</b>: <b>Arius</b>, scholar from <b>Alexandria</b> (center of learning)</li><li class=\"d1\">draws people off from the church, <b>taking them captive</b> with his doctrine → enslaved</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>fathers</b> with their position from God come to <b>rescue the church</b>, set it free</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not just, hey, look, <b>318</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Church's <b>self-understanding</b>: not <b>fine points of theology</b>, not <b>imperial power</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2619.7,"lv":3,"h":"Sheep and shepherds","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Unflattering\": we are <b>sheep</b> and have <b>shepherds</b> - \"I have a bishop... I have a shepherd\"; shepherds have a <b>chief shepherd</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Sheep <b>wander astray</b></li><li class=\"d1\">wander into a stream, <b>wool absorbs water</b>, they <b>drown standing there</b> - too dumb to walk out</li><li class=\"d1\">like <b>sin</b> in our lives: \"too dumb to abandon it, we just stand there and drown\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Shepherds come, <b>working with God</b>, to <b>rescue us from ourselves</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Church discipline / correction</b> from above → not an <b>attack</b>, but <b>concern, rescue, being set free</b> from what captured us</li><li class=\"d1\">\"for our <b>benefit</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Stopping here, mid-chapter: <b>Melchizedek</b> next - \"might be a long discussion\"</li></ul>"}],"next":"Next week: <b>Genesis 14:17</b> - Melchizedek"});
